"78_exceptions.mst — E0 + E1 of docs/exceptions_design.md.

 E0: the class hierarchy — Exception, Error and its concrete kinds, Warning.
 E1: `on:do:` and `signal`, so a library can finally report a recoverable
 condition to its caller instead of calling `self error:` and killing the
 computation.

 ── How this works, and why it needs no VM change ──────────────────────────

 The design doc's §3 proposed a new frame-marker predicate in the unwinder.
 Building E1 showed that is not necessary for the non-resumable case, because
 MACVM already has the two pieces the job needs:

   * a HANDLER STACK can live in the world (this file's `Handlers`), so
     `signal` finds its handler by SEARCHING, without touching frames; and
   * NON-LOCAL RETURN already unwinds correctly from an arbitrary depth back
     to a specific home frame, running every `ensure:`/`ifCurtailed:` block
     in between — which is exactly what `on:do:` must do once a handler
     decides to return.

 So `on:do:` pushes `[ :ex | ^aHandlerBlock value: ex ]` — a block whose HOME
 is the `on:do:` activation. `signal` calls it; the `^` inside performs an
 ordinary non-local return out of `on:do:`, and the existing unwinder does
 the rest. No new primitive, no new marker kind, no change to the JIT's
 `PRIM_ACTIVATES_FRAME` set.

 It is also NOT one-pass, which matters for the future: the SEARCH does not
 unwind. `signal` finds the handler and calls it with the stack intact, so a
 handler that answers WITHOUT a `^` simply returns its value to `signal` —
 which is resumption (E3), reachable later without redesigning this.

 ── What is deliberately NOT here (E4) ─────────────────────────────────────

 The VM's own failures — `self error:`, doesNotUnderstand, division by zero —
 do NOT signal yet. They still terminate exactly as before, so this file is
 purely additive and every existing gate keeps its meaning. `on: ZeroDivide
 do: […]` therefore catches a ZeroDivide someone SIGNALS, not yet one the VM
 raises. Wiring those is E4, and it must keep the no-handler path
 byte-identical to today."

Object subclass: Exception [
    "handlerReturn / handlerRetry are the running handler's two escape
     routes, bound by `signal` from the handler-stack entry just before the
     handler runs (E2). They are per-EXCEPTION, not per-class, so a signal
     raised from inside a handler cannot clobber the outer one's routes —
     and `pass`, which deliberately re-signals the SAME exception outward,
     captures its own pair first."
    | messageText handlerReturn handlerRetry handlerResume |
    <classVars: Handlers RetryToken>

    "── the handler stack ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One entry per active `on:do:`, innermost LAST. Held on Exception (the
     root) so every subclass shares one stack — a handler for Error must be
     found by a ZeroDivide signalled deeper in."

    Exception class >> handlers [
        Handlers isNil ifTrue: [ Handlers := OrderedCollection new ].
        ^Handlers
    ]

    "Push (exceptionClass, block) and answer the depth to unwind to. The
     DEPTH, not an index into a list that may have grown — `popHandlerTo:`
     truncates back to it, so an abandoned frame's entries cannot leak even
     if a non-local return skipped their own pops."
    Exception class >> pushHandler: aClass block: aBlock [
        ^self pushHandler: aClass block: aBlock return: nil retry: nil
    ]
    "An entry is (class, handlerBlock, returnBlock, retryBlock). The two
     blocks are created INSIDE the protected activation, so their `^`
     unwinds to it — which is the whole mechanism behind return:/retry."
    Exception class >> pushHandler: aClass block: aBlock return: aReturnBlock retry: aRetryBlock [
        self handlers add:
            (Array with: aClass with: aBlock with: aReturnBlock with: aRetryBlock).
        ^self handlers size - 1
    ]

    "The entries from `anIndex` to the top, in order — captured before a
     signal pops them so a resume can put them back (see signal)."
    Exception class >> handlersFrom: anIndex [
        | hs out |
        hs := self handlers.
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        anIndex to: hs size do: [ :i | out add: (hs at: i) ].
        ^out
    ]
    Exception class >> restoreHandlers: aCollection [
        aCollection do: [ :e | self handlers add: e ]
    ]

    "A private singleton meaning 'the handler asked to retry'. Compared with
     ==, and it never escapes this file — a handler answering it by accident
     is not a thing that can happen."
    Exception class >> retryToken [
        RetryToken isNil ifTrue: [ RetryToken := Object new ].
        ^RetryToken
    ]

    "Truncate back to `aDepth`. Idempotent and total: called from an
     `ensure:`, so it must be correct after a normal return, after a
     non-local return through it, and after a curtailment."
    Exception class >> popHandlerTo: aDepth [
        Handlers isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        [ Handlers size > aDepth ] whileTrue: [ Handlers removeLast ]
    ]

    "The innermost handler whose class this exception is a kind of, as
     (index, entry), or nil. Searched innermost-first — the ANSI rule."
    Exception class >> findHandlerFor: anException [
        | hs i |
        hs := self handlers.
        i := hs size.
        [ i >= 1 ] whileTrue: [
            | entry |
            entry := hs at: i.
            (anException isKindOf: (entry at: 1)) ifTrue: [
                ^Array with: i with: entry ].
            i := i - 1 ].
        ^nil
    ]

    "── construction and text ────────────────────────────────────────────"

    Exception class >> new ^ <Exception> [ ^self basicNew ]

    "Signal a fresh instance of me — `ZeroDivide signal` and
     `Error signal: 'text'` are the two forms library code uses."
    Exception class >> signal ^ <Object> [ ^self new signal ]
    Exception class >> signal: aString <String> ^ <Object> [
        ^(self new messageText: aString) signal
    ]

    messageText ^ <Object> [ ^messageText ]
    messageText: aString <String> ^ <Exception> [ messageText := aString. ^self ]

    "What a report shows. Subclasses override `description` for a default
     when no messageText was given; messageText always wins if present."
    description ^ <String> [ ^'An exception occurred' ]

    printOn: aStream [
        aStream nextPutAll: self class name asString.
        messageText isNil ifFalse: [
            aStream nextPutAll: ': '; nextPutAll: messageText ]
    ]

    "The text a report should show: the specific message when one was given,
     else the class's own description."
    report ^ <String> [
        ^messageText isNil ifTrue: [ self description ] ifFalse: [ messageText ]
    ]

    "── signalling ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Search innermost-first for a handler. While it runs, the handler and
     everything inside it are POPPED, so a signal raised by the handler
     itself cannot re-enter the same handler (an infinite loop) and instead
     finds the next one outward — the ANSI rule, and the same shape the
     unwinder's `curtailing_on_error` guard uses for `ensure:`.

     The handler block is `on:do:`'s wrapper, whose `^` returns from the
     `on:do:` activation; so in the ordinary case this send does not return
     here at all. If the handler answers WITHOUT a non-local return, its
     value is answered from `signal` — the resumption path (E3)."
    signal ^ <Object> [
        | found depth entry result popped |
        found := Exception findHandlerFor: self.
        found isNil ifTrue: [ ^self unhandled ].
        depth := (found at: 1) - 1.
        entry := found at: 2.
        "Bind this handler's escape routes onto me BEFORE it runs — return:,
         retry and pass all reach the protected activation through them."
        handlerReturn := entry at: 3.
        handlerRetry := entry at: 4.
        "E3: the resume route. Its `^` returns from THIS signal activation,
         which is the whole trick — pass 1 never unwound, so the signalling
         frame is still live and answering here continues it. Nothing else in
         the system can offer this, and it is why E1 chose two-pass.

         Resumption also carries an obligation the other verbs do not: we are
         about to pop this handler and every inner one, but resuming returns
         INSIDE all of them, where they must still be armed. So the popped
         slice is captured first and put back on the way out — otherwise a
         second signal after a resume would find no handler."
        popped := Exception handlersFrom: depth + 1.
        handlerResume := [ :v | Exception restoreHandlers: popped. ^v ].
        Exception popHandlerTo: depth.
        result := (entry at: 2) value: self.
        "The handler fell off its end. ANSI's default action is to `return:`
         its value — which under E1 was spelled as the `^` inside on:do:'s
         wrapper block. Making it explicit here is what let the wrapper go
         away, and with it the reason return: could not be written."
        ^self return: result
    ]


    "No handler matched. v1 keeps EXACTLY the old behaviour — the computation
     stops with a reported error — so a world that signals but never handles
     is no worse off than one that called `self error:` directly, and every
     existing gate keeps its meaning."
    unhandled ^ <Object> [
        "asString on the class name: it is a Symbol, and Symbols are
         immutable — , would try to build in place and raise inside the
         error reporter itself (found by testing the unhandled path)."
        "primError:, NOT error: — E4 makes error: signal, and signalling from
         inside the no-handler path would recurse forever. This send is also
         the exact point where E4 is behaviour-preserving: primitive 95 runs
         here, at the same moment it always did."
        "`self report`, NOT the class name plus it: primitive 95 already
         prefixes 'Error: ', so including the class here doubled the prefix —
         and, more importantly, E4 routes every VM failure through this
         point, where `error: 'division by zero'` must still print exactly
         `Error: division by zero` as it always did. The class identity is
         not lost: an exception signalled with no messageText reports its
         own `description` ('Division by zero'), and the raise site is in
         the trace either way."
        ^self primError: self report
    ]

    "Is the receiver resumable? Nothing resumes in E1; the accessor exists so
     E3 has one place to change and so handlers can ask honestly."
    isResumable ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
]

"── the standard kinds ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 Deliberately small. Each exists because the world's own library already
 fails in exactly this way and currently has no way to say so."

Exception subclass: Error [
    description ^ <String> [ ^'An error occurred' ]
]

Error subclass: ZeroDivide [
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Division by zero' ]
]

Error subclass: MessageNotUnderstood [
    | message receiver |
    message ^ <Object> [ ^message ]
    message: m <Object> ^ <MessageNotUnderstood> [ message := m. ^self ]
    receiver ^ <Object> [ ^receiver ]
    receiver: r <Object> ^ <MessageNotUnderstood> [ receiver := r. ^self ]
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Message not understood' ]
]

Error subclass: KeyNotFound [
    | key |
    key ^ <Object> [ ^key ]
    key: k <Object> ^ <KeyNotFound> [ key := k. ^self ]
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Key not found' ]
]

Error subclass: IndexOutOfBounds [
    | index |
    index ^ <Object> [ ^index ]
    index: i <Object> ^ <IndexOutOfBounds> [ index := i. ^self ]
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Index out of bounds' ]
]

Error subclass: InvalidArgument [
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Invalid argument' ]
]

"Resumable by design — the ANSI distinction that keeps the dangerous case
 opt-in. Nothing resumes until E3; `isResumable` answering true here is the
 declaration, not the mechanism."
Exception subclass: Warning [
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Warning' ]
    isResumable ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ]

    "An unhandled Warning REPORTS and CARRIES ON, answering nil — the ANSI
     default action for a resumable exception, and the thing that makes a
     Warning worth having: a warning that killed the computation when nobody
     was listening would just be an Error with a friendlier name.

     This is the one place E3 changes what an unhandled signal does, and it
     is safe to do here because Warning is new in E0 and nothing in the world
     signals one yet — the compatibility hinge (E4: VM errors behave exactly
     as today when unhandled) is untouched."
    unhandled ^ <Object> [
        Transcript showCr: 'Warning: ' , self report.
        ^nil
    ]
]

"── E2: the handler verbs ────────────────────────────────────────────────

 Re-opened AFTER the subclasses, because these methods name `Error` and the
 loader resolves globals at compile time — inside Exception's own block,
 Error does not exist yet. (The same reason the file re-opens BlockClosure
 below rather than declaring it early.)"

Object subclass: Exception [
    "── E2: the handler verbs (docs/exceptions_design.md E2) ─────────────
     Each reaches the protected activation through a block created there, so
     each is one non-local return — no unwinder change and no new primitive,
     exactly as E1 needed none."

    "Abandon the handler; `on:do:` answers aValue. The explicit form of what
     a handler that simply ends already does."
    return: aValue <Object> ^ <Object> [
        handlerReturn isNil ifTrue: [ ^Error signal: 'return: outside a handler' ].
        ^handlerReturn value: aValue
    ]
    return ^ <Object> [ ^self return: nil ]

    "Abandon the handler and run the protected block AGAIN with the handler
     re-armed. The retry block answers a private token that on:do:'s loop
     recognises, so this is a genuine loop rather than recursion — an
     unbounded retry (a reconnect loop is the motivating case) costs no
     stack."
    retry ^ <Object> [
        handlerRetry isNil ifTrue: [ ^Error signal: 'retry outside a handler' ].
        ^handlerRetry value
    ]

    "Decline: hand this same exception to the NEXT handler outward and let
     its action stand. The stack is already truncated past our own entry (see
     signal), so re-signalling searches strictly outward and cannot re-enter
     us. Our own return route is captured FIRST, because signalling rebinds
     it to whichever handler catches next; it matters only if that outer
     handler resumes (E3), the one path that comes back here."
    pass ^ <Object> [
        | mine |
        mine := handlerReturn.
        mine isNil ifTrue: [ ^Error signal: 'pass outside a handler' ].
        ^mine value: self signal
    ]

    "Like pass, but if the outer handler RESUMES, control returns to THIS
     handler with its value instead of leaving — the difference E3 makes
     real."
    outer ^ <Object> [ ^self signal ]

    "── E3: resumption (docs/exceptions_design.md E3) ────────────────────

     Abandon the handler and CONTINUE THE SIGNALLING EXPRESSION, which
     answers aValue. `Warning signal` in the middle of a computation becomes
     an expression whose value the handler chooses.

     Gated on isResumable — the ANSI rule, and the reason Warning declares
     itself resumable while Error does not. Resuming an Error would return
     control to code that had just declared itself unable to continue, so
     the attempt is refused rather than obeyed."
    resume: aValue <Object> ^ <Object> [
        self isResumable ifFalse: [
            ^Error signal: self class name asString , ' is not resumable' ].
        handlerResume isNil ifTrue: [ ^Error signal: 'resume: outside a handler' ].
        ^handlerResume value: aValue
    ]
    resume ^ <Object> [ ^self resume: nil ]

    "Signal me with a message text — the instance-side twin of the class-side
     `Error signal:` that E0 specified and never got."
    signal: aString <String> ^ <Object> [
        messageText := aString.
        ^self signal
    ]
]

"── E1: the protected block ──────────────────────────────────────────────"

"Re-opened, the world's own idiom for adding methods to an existing class
 (59_reflection.mst does the same) — a redeclaration carrying only the new
 method, not a redefinition."
Object subclass: BlockClosure [
    "Run me, and if I signal an exception that is a kind of `anExceptionClass`,
     run `aHandlerBlock` with it and answer ITS value from this `on:do:`.

     The pushed wrapper's `^` is what does the returning: its home is THIS
     activation, so the existing non-local-return unwinder walks back from
     wherever the signal happened, running every `ensure:`/`ifCurtailed:`
     block in between, and lands here. The `ensure:` below pops the handler
     on every exit path — normal, non-local, or curtailed."
    on: anExceptionClass <Object> do: aHandlerBlock <[Object,^Object]> ^ <Object> [
        "A LOOP, so `retry` costs no stack however many times it fires. Each
         turn arms a fresh handler; the private token is the only value that
         means 'go round again', and it can only come from Exception>>retry."
        | r |
        [ true ] whileTrue: [
            r := self protect: anExceptionClass handler: aHandlerBlock.
            (r == Exception retryToken) ifFalse: [ ^r ] ]
    ]

    "One armed attempt. The return and retry blocks are created HERE, so
     their `^` unwinds to this activation — which is what makes `return:`
     answer from on:do: and `retry` reach the loop above. The handler block
     is pushed RAW now; E1 wrapped it as `[ :ex | ^... ]`, and that wrapper's
     job — answering the handler's value from on:do: — moved into signal's
     explicit default action, which is precisely what made the verbs
     expressible."
    protect: anExceptionClass <Object> handler: aHandlerBlock <[Object,^Object]> ^ <Object> [
        | depth |
        depth := Exception pushHandler: anExceptionClass
                           block: aHandlerBlock
                           return: [ :v | ^v ]
                           retry: [ ^Exception retryToken ].
        ^[ self value ] ensure: [ Exception popHandlerTo: depth ]
    ]
]
